# Difference-in-differences and the parallel trends assumption
subtitle: "What a two-by-two difference-in-differences subtracts, why its causal claim rests on a line nobody sees, and what the 2014 Medicaid expansions did to deaths."
tags: ["difference-in-differences","parallel trends","medicaid expansion","event study","health insurance"]
teaches: ["methods/difference-in-differences","methods/parallel-trends","methods/event-studies"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"When treatment arrives at different times: staggered DiD in trouble","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/when-treatment-arrives-at-different-times-staggered-did-in-trouble.cast"}

## Two changes, one subtraction
