    text d_t "−12" #2f6b8f at.data [2013.55,25.8] font_size 40 hidden true
    text d_c "−8" #b5482e at.data [2013.5,38.3] font_size 40 hidden true
    text note_a "Marketplaces" #8f887c at.data [2013.75,45] font_size 34 hidden true
    text note_b "Mandate" #8f887c at.data [2014.75,45] font_size 34 hidden true
    text note_c "Jobs" #8f887c at.data [2015.55,45] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Two changes, one subtraction"

(@draw ax@) In 2014 the share of poor adults without insurance fell in the American state (@draw yl@)s that expanded Medicaid. But it also fell in the states that didn' (@draw x13 x15@)t. So how much of the fall was Medicaid's doing?

Expanding was a choice: a Supreme Court ruling in 2012 let each state decide, and about half said yes from January 2014.
    highlight x13 x15 effect underline

Start with low-income adults in the states that expanded. Our numbers are round and illustrative: thirty-three percent uninsured in 2013.
    draw pt13 v_t13

Two years later, twenty-one percent.
    draw t_line pt15 v_t15 n_t

The ob (@highlight t_line@)vious answer is the whole fall:  (@draw d_t@)twelve percentage points.

But 2014 brought more than Medicaid: subsi (@draw note_a@)dised private plans, a rule that ev (@draw note_b@)eryone be insured, and jobs  (@draw note_c@)coming back, in nearly every state.

So look at the states that did not expand: thirty-seven percent uninsured in 2013, twenty-nine in 2015.
    draw pc13 v_c13 c_line pc15 v_c15 n_c

They fell eight points with no expansion at all, from everything else that 2014 brought.
    draw d_c

    erase note_a note_b note_c

If the expansion states had gone through the same eight-point fall, they would have landed at twenty-five.
    draw cf_line v_cf

They landed at twenty-one. The four points between the two are the estimate of what expanding did.
    arrow eff #8a5fa8 from.x 2015 from.y 24.7 to.x 2015 to.y 21.5 style.stroke_width 4
    text v_eff "−4" #8a5fa8 at.data [2015.22,23] font_size 40

Written out, it is the change in the expansion states minus the change in the others: a difference in differences.
    math f_did at.data [2014.6,46] tex "\\hat\\tau = ({21} - {33}) - ({29} - {37})" colors {"{21}":"#2f6b8f","{33}":"#2f6b8f","{29}":"#b5482e","{37}":"#b5482e","\\hat\\tau":"#8a5fa8","{-12}":"#2f6b8f","{-8}":"#b5482e","{-4}":"#8a5fa8"} font_size 40

Minus twelve, less minus eight, leaves minus four: four points fewer uninsured.
    morph f_did tex "\\hat\\tau = {-12} - ({-8}) = {-4}"

The two groups never started level: the others were four poin (@highlight v_t13 v_c13@)ts worse off in 2013, and the method doesn't mind.
    arrow gap13 thick #8f887c from.x 2012.88 from.y 33.4 to.x 2012.88 to.y 36.6

A steady gap between groups cancels out in the subtraction; only the changes count.
    highlight d_t d_c

    quiz "Suppose the states that did not expand had fallen only from 37 to 33, while the expansion states still went from 33 to 21. What is the estimate?" intro "Try new numbers."
        right: Minus eight: the expansion states fell twelve, the others four, and the estimate is the difference between those two falls.
        wrong: Work out how far the others fell first, then take that from the expansion states' fall.
        * Minus 12 points
        + Minus 8 points
        * Minus 4 points

But every step of that rested on one dashed line, and nobody has ever seen it.
    highlight cf_line color #f2c14e

## The line nobody sees
