    math f_bias tex "\\hat\\tau = {\\mathrm{ATT}} + {B}" colors {"\\hat\\tau":"#8a5fa8","{\\mathrm{ATT}}":"#8a5fa8","{B}":"#8f887c"} x 850 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true
    text r_est "Estimate: −4" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 440 font_size 38 hidden true
    text r_att "True effect: {att:0}" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 340 font_size 38 hidden true
    text r_b "Bias: {bias:0}" #8f887c x 850 y 240 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "The line nobody sees"

(@draw ax yl x13 x15 pt13 pc13 v_t13 v_c13 t_line c_line pt15 pc15 v_t15 v_c15 n_t n_c parallel true@) Four points fewer uninsured: that answer rests on a guess, that without expanding, t (@draw cf_line pcf v_cf eff@)hose states would have fallen just as far as the others.

Call living in an expansion state D equals one, and write Y-zero for the uninsured rate a group would have had without expanding.
    draw f_pt

For the states that did not exp (@highlight f_pt part "{E[Y_{2015}(0) - Y_{2013}(0) \\mid D=0]}" effect underline color #b5482e@)and, the change in Y-zero is simply what happened: their fall of eight.
    highlight c_line

For the expansion states, Y-zero in 2015 never happe (@highlight f_pt part "{E[Y_{2015}(0) - Y_{2013}(0) \\mid D=1]}" effect underline color #d0865f@)ned. It is the dashed line, with its question mark.
    highlight cf_line color #f2c14e

Parallel trends says those two changes are equal on average. Not the levels: the changes.
    highlight f_pt effect underline color #3d3833

    erase f_pt

(@draw f_bias@) If the assumption fails, the estimate is the tr (@draw r_est@)ue average effect on the expansion stat (@draw r_att@)es, the ATT, plus a bias, B: the gap between th (@draw r_b@)e two untreated trends.

Right now the dashed line falls eight poin (@highlight r_b@)ts, so the bias is zero, and the true effect eq (@highlight r_att r_est@)uals the estimate: minus four.
    highlight cf_line color #f2c14e

Grab the dashed line and pull its end down, as if those states would have fallen faster on their own, and watch the true effect.
    explore params ["s"] watch "Watch the dashed line fall faster: the true effect shrinks and the bias grows, while the estimate stays at minus four."

At an eleven-point fall, the true effect is only minus one. (@highlight r_att r_b@) The other three points are bias, and the estimate st (@highlight r_est@)ill says minus four.
    animate s 11 duration 3

And nothing in the 2013 and 2015 numbers can te (@highlight pt13 pt15 pc13 pc15@)ll those two worlds apart: the data are the same in both.
    animate s 8 duration 2

    erase f_bias r_est r_att r_b

So researchers look back, to the years before anyone expanded.
    draw x10 pt_p0 pc_p0 pt_p1 pc_p1 pt_p2 pc_p2 t_pre c_pre

Here both groups drift down half a point a year, side by side, from 2010 to 2013.
    highlight t_pre c_pre

But the assumption is about 2015, after t (@point at.ref pcf gesture circle@)he expansion, and no earlier year can reach that dashed line.

    quiz "From 2010 to 2013 the two groups moved in parallel. What does that show?"
        right: It shows the groups moved together before 2014. That rules out some bad stories, but the assumption is about the years after.
        wrong: Ask which years the assumption is about, and which years we looked at.
        * That parallel trends holds after 2014
        * Nothing useful at all
        + No sign of diverging trends before 2014

Parallel lines before a policy are a failed attempt to refute the ass (@highlight cf_line color #f2c14e@)umption, never a proof of it.
    draw pre_note

And real data are noisy in every year, so even the past can hide a slow drift between the groups.
    highlight pt_p0 pt_p1 pt_p2 pc_p0 pc_p1 pc_p2

## What the expansions did to deaths
