sources:
    - id: mjw2021
      title: 'Medicaid and Mortality: New Evidence from Linked Survey and Administrative Data'
      authors: Sarah Miller, Norman Johnson, Laura R. Wherry, Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2021
      finding: 'US citizens aged 55-64 in 2014 with low income or education (about 566,000 ACS respondents linked to death records, 2008-2017; early-expansion states excluded): ACA Medicaid expansion lowered annual mortality by 0.132 percentage points (SE 0.050), 9.4% of the sample mean; uninsurance fell 4.4 points (SE 1.0); event-study pre-period coefficients near zero; no effect among people 65+; exploratory cause-of-death analysis points to disease-related deaths.'
    - id: black2022
      title: 'Simulated power analyses for observational studies: An application to the Affordable Care Act Medicaid expansion'
      authors: Bernard Black, Alex Hollingsworth, Leticia Nunes, Kosali Simon, Journal of Public Economics 213:104713 (NBER WP 25568, 2019)
      year: 2022
      finding: No statistically significant evidence of an ACA-induced mortality decline in expansion states, with wide confidence intervals; DiD on county or state mortality data would need population mortality reductions of at least about 2% to be well powered.
    - id: roth2022
      title: 'Pretest with Caution: Event-Study Estimates after Testing for Parallel Trends'
      authors: 'Jonathan Roth, American Economic Review: Insights'
      year: 2022
      finding: Conventional pre-trend tests often have low power against violations large enough to matter, and conditioning on passing them can worsen bias.
      doi: 10.1257/aeri.20210236
    - id: finkelstein2012
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group), Quarterly Journal of Economics
      year: 2012
      finding: Lottery for Medicaid among about 90,000 sign-ups, about 30,000 drawn; first-year effects on use, financial strain and self-reported health; no statistically significant effect on mortality.
takeaways:
    - Subtract changes, not levels
    - 'Parallel trends: the unseen line'
    - Flat pre-trends refute, never prove

