    card title "What the expansions did to deaths"

(@draw ax yl ym2 yp2 x2008 x2010 x2012 x2014 x2016@) Fewer uninsured is one thing. Did expanding Medicaid keep people alive? Sarah Miller, Norman Johnson and La (@draw tl tl_lab@)ura Wherry used this design to ask.

(@draw s_name@) They took more than half a million low-income or less-educated Americans aged f (@draw s_pop@)ifty-five to sixty-four from a large Census survey, and linked them to death records up to 2017.

Each dot is the gap (@draw d2008 w2008 d2009 w2009 d2010 w2010 d2011 w2011 d2012 w2012 d2013 parallel true@) in yearly deaths per thousand between expansion and non-expansion states,  (@highlight d2013 effect circle@)measured against 2013, with its ninety-five percent range. Before  (@highlight w2008 w2009 w2010 w2011 w2012@)2014 they hover around zero.

After, the (@draw d2014 w2014 d2015 w2015 d2016 w2016 d2017 w2017 parallel true@) gap opens year by year: almost one death fewer per thousand in 2014, about two fewer by 2017.

Their summary e (@draw e_val@)stimate: one point three fewer deaths per thousand a (@draw e_pct@) year, about nine percent, with a range from  (@draw e_ci@)about point three to two point three.

That is the effect of expanding on the whole group, most of whom never enrolled, so if it worked only through those who gained M (@highlight e_pct@)edicaid, their own effect was about seven times larger.
    highlight e_val

    erase s_pop

But the early bars are wide: a drift of a fifth of a death per (@draw drift@) thousand a year fits inside every one, and carried forward could explai (@highlight drift color #f2c14e@)n a third to three-quarters of the estimate.
    highlight w2008 w2009 w2010 w2011 w2012

And the usual test would no (@draw n_roth@)t catch it: Jonathan Roth showed in 2022 that pre-trend tests in published studi (@highlight n_roth@)es often miss drifts big enough to matter, so passing one proves little.

    erase drift n_roth

So what story could brea (@highlight tl@)k parallel trends here?
    draw n_q

    erase n_q

Expansion stat (@draw n_half@)es were not a random half. Their low-income adults were a litt (@highlight n_half@)le better off, and fewer of them were uninsured to begin with.

    erase n_half

So anything else that moved t (@draw n_shock@)heir death rates from 2014, a faster recovery, a different turn in the overdose  (@highlight d2014 d2015 d2016 d2017@)crisis, would be booked as Medicaid.

    erase n_shock

The authors lo (@draw n_plac@)oked where such a shock would show but Medicaid could  (@highlight d2008 d2009 d2010 d2011 d2012@)not: people already sixty-five, on Medicare. Th (@highlight n_plac@)eir gap stayed flat.

    erase n_plac

Not everyone is per (@draw n_black@)suaded. Bernard Black and colleagues, working with county death counts, found no statistically cl (@highlight d2014 d2015 d2016 d2017@)ear fall, and showed that designs like theirs can only see very large effects.

    erase n_black

What separates the t (@draw n_tell@)wo readings is precision and checks: records on each person who could  (@highlight e_ci@)gain Medicaid, the flat gap for the over-sixty-fives, and a fall in de (@highlight n_tell@)aths from disease rather than accidents.

    erase n_tell

Oregon's 2008 lott (@draw n_or@)ery, which offered Medicaid by chance to some of about ninety thousand applicants, needs no such assumption. But it was too s (@highlight n_or@)mall and too short to settle deaths.

    quiz "A study compares hospital deaths in states that raised nurse staffing in 2016 with states that did not. Which finding would worry you most?" intro "One new case."
        right: The trauma centres arrive with the policy, in the same states, so they move the untreated path itself, and the double difference books them as the staffing effect.
        wrong: A steady gap in levels cancels out. Look for something that changed in only one group, at the same time.
        + The staffing states also opened trauma centres in 2016
        * The staffing states had higher death rates all along
        * Death rates fell in both groups

    erase n_or s_name s_pop e_val e_ci e_pct

So how much of the fall was Medicaid's? The double difference answers by taking  (@highlight d2014 d2015 d2016 d2017@)the others' change from the expansion states' change.
    text takeaway_1 "Subtract changes,\nnot levels" #3d3833 x 850 y 540 font_size 34

That answer is causal only if, untreated, both groups would have move (@highlight tl@)d alike: a claim about a line no one ever saw.
    text takeaway_2 "Parallel trends:\nthe unseen line" #3d3833 x 850 y 390 font_size 34

Flat years before can refute that claim but never prove it, so  (@highlight w2008 w2009 w2010 w2011 w2012@)a good study argues for the line with every check it can find.
    text takeaway_3 "Flat pre-trends\nrefute, never prove" #3d3833 x 850 y 240 font_size 34

